# POISONED FIXTURE unit 2 of 6 — expected outcome: CRASHED (status 139, SIGSEGV)
#
# HONESTY NOTE — read this before believing the label.
#
# There is no known .spl source that makes the compiler SIGSEGV on demand, and
# inventing one would pin the fixture to a bug that will be fixed out from under
# it. So the source here is ordinary and valid; the SEGV is induced at the
# PROCESS level by the harness, which runs this unit's build command as
# `sh -c 'kill -SEGV $$'`.
#
# What that does and does not prove:
#   DOES prove — the supervisor observes a REAL wait status of 139 from a REAL
#     child killed by a REAL SIGSEGV, classifies it CRASHED, and keeps going.
#   Does NOT prove — that the Simple compiler itself segfaults on this input.
#     It does not. The unit under test is the supervisor, not the parser.

fn crashing_unit_two(n: i64) -> i64:
    n * 2
